#{[17592186484222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484222 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $w$ satisfy the inequality $2\\le 5w\\le 15\\,?$\n\nI. $w=1$\n\nII. $w=2$\n\nIII. $w=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54c177e7-bfd3-5330-bd52-c67fa05a6194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484222 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=1$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 1 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=1$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=1$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 2 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 10 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=3$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 3 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 15 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=3$ **satisfies** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1069 "q-63544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $v$ satisfy the inequality $7v\\le 28\\,?$\n\nI. $v=1$\n\nII. $v=3$\n\nIII. $v=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78ade5bf-b2b7-57c1-a1d4-ab36482d20cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484237 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $v=1$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7v & \\le 28 \\\\ 7\\cdot 1 & \\le 28 \\\\ 7 & \\le 28\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $v=1$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $v=1$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $v=3$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7v & \\le 28 \\\\ 7\\cdot 3 & \\le 28 \\\\ 21 & \\le 28\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $v=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $v=3$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $v=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7v & \\le 28 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4 & \\le 28 \\\\ 28 & \\le 28\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $v=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $v=4$ **satisfies** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{5t}{2}\\ge 15\\,?$\n\nI. $t=4$\n\nII. $t=6$\n\nIII. $t=8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ec4ef84-8b35-542a-b22f-98607c9a4fdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484242 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 10 & \\ge 15\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=4$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=6$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 6}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 15 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=6$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=8$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 8}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{40}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 20 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=8$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=8$ **satisfies** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $x=4$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $x-3>0$\n\nII. $2x\\le 10$\n\nIII. $1<x+5<6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b784d723-3b79-553d-9de5-8ee50049511b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484268 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 \\\\ 4-3 & >0 \\\\ 1 & >0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x & \\le 10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & \\le 10 \\\\ 8 & \\le 10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<4+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<9 & <6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $7t\\ge 19\\,?$\n\nI. $t=2$\n\nII. $t=3$\n\nIII. $t=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba00c538-8a3f-5eb5-9ed3-b95b47d4f897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484269 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 14 & \\ge 19\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=2$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=3$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 3 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 21 & \\ge 19\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=3$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 28 & \\ge 19\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=4$ **satisfies** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $z=0$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $z+3\\ge 1$\n\nII. $3z+1<0$\n\nIII. $-6\\le \\frac{z}{2}-1\\le -2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cad781ca-8e12-5b8d-a5a5-787d8ffa6e99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484274 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+3 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 0+3 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 3 & \\ge 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+1 & <0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 0+1 & <0 \\\\ 1 & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\le \\frac{z}{2}-1 & \\le -2 \\\\ -6\\le \\frac{0}{2}-1 & \\le -2 \\\\ -6\\le -1 & \\le -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **false** statement.  \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $y=-1$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $2<y+1<8$\n\nII. $4y<5$\n\nIII. $2\\le y+3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed3aeee5-2189-5d52-8e31-431c161f29f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484290 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $y=-1$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<y+1 & <8 \\\\ 2<-1+1 & <8 \\\\ 2<0 & <8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $y=-1$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $y=-1$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $y=-1$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & <5 \\\\ 4\\cdot (-1) & <5 \\\\ -4 & <5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $y=-1$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $y=-1$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $y=-1$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & \\le y+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le -1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le 2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $y=-1$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $y=-1$ **satisfies** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
